  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Time-Domain Signal from $X(s)$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Reconstruct the causal time-domain signal from a solved $X(s)$ by decomposing it into known one-sided Laplace pairs and converting each term back to its time-domain component.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Time-Domain Signal from $X(s)$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Reconstructing a Time-Domain Signal from $X(s)$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Reconstruction uses a one-sided inverse-Laplace move that is only valid for causal terms. For this lesson, a term is treated as causal when it matches the form that already includes or implies $u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For each $X(s)$ term, first identify the corresponding one-sided pair, then apply inverse mapping term-by-term. Use linearity: multiply, add, and subtract in $X(s)$ first, then invert each piece into the same operation pattern for $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The base-case anchor is $X(s)=1/(s+3)$. The move is to read this as the transform of an exponential decay with no shifts and no extra constants, so the reconstructed time signal is $x(t)=e^{-3t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{1}{s+a} \\Rightarrow x(t)=e^{-at}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next, move from one known term to one-piece combinations. The same reconstruction rule works on sums and constants, so each part is inverted independently and then added in the time domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=X_1(s)+X_2(s) \\Rightarrow x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the next sections you will do this as a pattern: first a single known pair, then a sum of known pairs, then delayed terms, and finally a full reconstructed signal from a full solved $X(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Time-Domain Signal from $X(s)$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Reconstructing from a Single Known One-Sided Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Reconstruct the causal signal from $X(s)=\\frac{3}{s+4}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a single one-sided rational term with the denominator in the form $s+a$, so this is a direct pair-recognition case. Identify the base pair $\\frac{1}{s+a} \\leftrightarrow e^{-at}u(t)$ and read $a=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}_+\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+4}\\right\\}=e^{-4t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the numerator gain outside the inverse transform. Since $X(s)=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4}$, multiply the time-domain map by $3$ without changing the exponential rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}_+\\left\\{\\frac{3}{s+4}\\right\\}=3\\,\\mathcal{L}^{-1}_+\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+4}\\right\\}=3e^{-4t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the reconstructed causal signal is $x(t)=3e^{-4t}u(t)$, which matches the one-sided target of a scaled decaying exponential starting at $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Time-Domain Signal from $X(s)$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Reconstructing from a Sum of Known One-Sided Pairs",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the causal signal for $X(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+4}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a sum of two known one-sided pairs, so apply linearity first. Write $X(s)$ as separate terms instead of trying to invert the whole fraction stack at once.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Split the transform into pieces: $X(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+4}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}_+\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+a}\\right\\}=e^{-at}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $a=1$, $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}_+\\{1/(s+1)\\}=e^{-t}u(t)$, then scale by $2$. For $a=4$, $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}_+\\{1/(s+4)\\}=e^{-4t}u(t)$. Keep each output causal with $u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2e^{-t}u(t)+e^{-4t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The reconstructed signal is the sum of the inverted pieces, so the final result is $x(t)=2e^{-t}u(t)+e^{-4t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Time-Domain Signal from $X(s)$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Reconstructing Delayed Terms from Time-Shifting Factors",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $x(t)$ for $X(s)=\\frac{4}{s+1}e^{-2s}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a single delayed one-sided pair: the factor $e^{-2s}$ indicates a right shift in time after inversion. Write $X(s)=e^{-2s}X_0(s)$ with $X_0(s)=\\frac{4}{s+1}$ and invert $X_0(s)$ first.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}_+\\left\\{\\frac{4}{s+1}\\right\\}=4e^{-t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the one-sided delay rule with $T=2$: $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{e^{-sT}X_0(s)\\}=x_0(t-T)u(t-T)$. Here that gives $x(t)=4e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{4}{s+1}e^{-2s}\\right\\}=4e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The sign is fixed by causality: delay is $t-2$ inside the exponential and $u(t-2)$ in the support, not $t+2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Time-Domain Signal from $X(s)$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reconstructing a Full Causal Signal from a Solved $X(s)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $X(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}+\\frac{3}{s+2}e^{-2s}+\\frac{1}{s+4}$, find the causal time-domain signal.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The target is a full decomposition: identify three terms, apply linearity, and invert each with the one-sided map. Write $X(s)=X_1(s)+X_2(s)+X_3(s)$, where $X_1(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}$, $X_2(s)=\\frac{3}{s+2}e^{-2s}$, and $X_3(s)=\\frac{1}{s+4}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the first and third non-delayed terms, invert directly using $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{1/(s+a)\\}=e^{-at}u(t)$. Multiply by numerator gains before recombining.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}_+\\left\\{\\frac{2}{s+1}\\right\\}=2e^{-t}u(t),\\quad\\mathcal{L}^{-1}_+\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+4}\\right\\}=e^{-4t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The middle term has a delay factor $e^{-2s}$, so invert $\\frac{3}{s+2}$ first, then apply the shift rule to produce $u(t-2)$ support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}_+\\left\\{\\frac{3}{s+2}e^{-2s}\\right\\}=3e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Add the three reconstructed pieces: $x(t)=2e^{-t}u(t)+3e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)+e^{-4t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
